<article class="problem-card">

<div class="problem-header">

    <div class="problem-number">
        PROBLEM 01
    </div>

    <h2>
        Transmission through a finite barrier for E>V_0
    </h2>

</div>


<section class="section">

<div class="section-title">
    Question Statement
</div>

<p>
Derive Equation (2.37) and plot the transmission coefficient
T as a function of the incident-particle energy E.
Comment on the behavior of T.
</p>

</section>


<section class="section">

<div class="section-title">
    Concept
</div>

<div class="concept-box">

<p>
For the finite barrier:
</p>

<div class="equation">

\[
V(x)=
\begin{cases}
0,&x<0,\\
V_0,&0\le x\le a,\\
0,&x>a.
\end{cases}
\]

</div>

<p>
For E>V_0, the wave is oscillatory in all three regions.
Define
</p>

<div class="equation">

\[
k=\frac{\sqrt{2mE}}{\hbar},
\qquad
q=\frac{\sqrt{2m(E-V_0)}}{\hbar}.
\]

</div>

</div>

</section>


<section class="section">

<div class="section-title">
    Complete Solution
</div>


<div class="step">

<h4>Step 1 — Region I</h4>

<div class="equation">

\[
\psi_1=Ae^{ikx}+Be^{-ikx}
\]

</div>

<p>
The first term is the incident wave and the second is
the reflected wave.
</p>

</div>


<div class="step">

<h4>Step 2 — Region II</h4>

<div class="equation">

\[
\psi_2=Ce^{iqx}+De^{-iqx}
\]

</div>

</div>


<div class="step">

<h4>Step 3 — Region III</h4>

<div class="equation">

\[
\psi_3=Fe^{ikx}
\]

</div>

<p>
There is no wave incident from +\infty.
</p>

</div>


<div class="step">

<h4>Step 4 — Boundary conditions at x=0</h4>

<div class="equation">

\[
A+B=C+D
\]

</div>

<div class="equation">

\[
k(A-B)=q(C-D)
\]

</div>

</div>


<div class="step">

<h4>Step 5 — Boundary conditions at x=a</h4>

<div class="equation">

\[
Ce^{iqa}+De^{-iqa}=Fe^{ika}
\]

</div>

<div class="equation">

\[
q(Ce^{iqa}-De^{-iqa})
=
kFe^{ika}.
\]

</div>

</div>


<div class="step">

<h4>Step 6 — Amplitude ratio</h4>

<div class="equation">

\[
\frac{F}{A}
=
\frac{e^{-ika}}
{
\cos(qa)
-i
\left(
\frac{k^2+q^2}{2kq}
\right)
\sin(qa)
}.
\]

</div>

</div>


<div class="step">

<h4>Step 7 — Transmission coefficient</h4>

<p>
Because the potential is the same on both sides,
the incident and transmitted wave numbers are equal.
Therefore:
</p>

<div class="equation">

\[
T=
\left|\frac FA\right|^2.
\]

</div>

<p>
Hence:
</p>

<div class="final-equation">

\[
\boxed{
T=
\left[
1+
\frac{V_0^2}
{4E(E-V_0)}
\sin^2(qa)
\right]^{-1}
}
\]

</div>

<div class="equation">

\[
\boxed{
q=
\frac{\sqrt{2m(E-V_0)}}{\hbar}
}
\]

</div>

</div>


<div class="step">

<h4>Step 8 — Resonance</h4>

<div class="equation">

\[
qa=n\pi
\]

</div>

<p>
Then
</p>

<div class="equation">

\[
\sin^2(qa)=0
\]

</div>

<p>
and therefore
</p>

<div class="final-equation">

\[
\boxed{T=1}
\]

</div>

<p>
The resonance energies are:
</p>

<div class="equation">

\[
\boxed{
E_n=
V_0+
\frac{n^2\pi^2\hbar^2}{2ma^2}
}
\]

</div>

</div>


<div class="step">

<h4>Step 9 — High-energy behavior</h4>

<div class="equation">

\[
E\gg V_0
\quad\Rightarrow\quad
T\rightarrow1.
\]

</div>

<p>
Thus a highly energetic particle is transmitted almost completely.
</p>

</div>

</section>


<div class="physics-box">

<h3>Physical Interpretation</h3>

<p>
The particle wave is reflected at both barrier boundaries.
The reflected waves interfere with one another. At special
energies the interference produces complete transmission:
resonant transmission.
</p>

</div>


<div class="answer">

<h3>Final Answer</h3>

<div class="final-equation">

\[
\boxed{
T=
\left[
1+
\frac{V_0^2}
{4E(E-V_0)}
\sin^2(qa)
\right]^{-1}
}
\]

</div>

</div>

