Derive Equation (2.39) and plot the transmission coefficient
as a function of E.
</p>

</section>


<section class="section">

<div class="section-title">
Concept
</div>

<p>
For E<V_0, the region inside the barrier is classically
forbidden. Define:
</p>

<div class="equation">

\[
k=
\frac{\sqrt{2mE}}{\hbar}
\]

\[
\boxed{
\kappa=
\frac{\sqrt{2m(V_0-E)}}{\hbar}
}
\]

</div>

</section>


<section class="section">

<div class="section-title">
Complete Solution
</div>


<div class="step">

<h4>Step 1 — Wave functions</h4>

<div class="equation">

\[
\psi_1=Ae^{ikx}+Be^{-ikx}
\]

\[
\psi_2=Ce^{\kappa x}+De^{-\kappa x}
\]

\[
\psi_3=Fe^{ikx}
\]

</div>

</div>


<div class="step">

<h4>Step 2 — Boundary conditions</h4>

<div class="equation">

\[
\psi_1(0)=\psi_2(0)
\]

\[
\psi_1'(0)=\psi_2'(0)
\]

\[
\psi_2(a)=\psi_3(a)
\]

\[
\psi_2'(a)=\psi_3'(a)
\]

</div>

</div>


<div class="step">

<h4>Step 3 — Hyperbolic form</h4>

<p>
For E<V_0,
</p>

<div class="equation">

\[
q=i\kappa
\]

</div>

<p>
and
</p>

<div class="equation">

\[
\sin(i\kappa a)
=
i\sinh(\kappa a).
\]

</div>

</div>


<div class="step">

<h4>Step 4 — Transmission coefficient</h4>

<div class="final-equation">

\[
\boxed{
T=
\left[
1+
\frac{V_0^2}
{4E(V_0-E)}
\sinh^2(\kappa a)
\right]^{-1}
}
\]

</div>

</div>


<div class="step">

<h4>Step 5 — Thick barrier approximation</h4>

<p>
For
</p>

<div class="equation">

\[
\kappa a\gg1
\]

</div>

<p>
we have
</p>

<div class="equation">

\[
\sinh(\kappa a)
\approx
\frac12e^{\kappa a}.
\]

</div>

<p>
Therefore approximately:
</p>

<div class="final-equation">

\[
\boxed{
T\propto e^{-2\kappa a}
}
\]

</div>

</div>

</section>


<div class="physics-box">

<h3>Physical Interpretation</h3>

<p>
Classically a particle with E<V_0 cannot cross the barrier.
Quantum mechanically the wave function penetrates the forbidden
region and produces a nonzero transmission probability.
This is quantum tunneling.
</p>

</div>

</article>



