</h2>

</div>


<section class="section">

<div class="section-title">
Question Statement
</div>

<p>
For the ground state and first two excited states of the
one-dimensional simple harmonic oscillator, find the probability
for the particle to be beyond the classical turning points.
</p>

</section>


<section class="section">

<div class="section-title">
Concept
</div>

<div class="equation">

\[
E_n=
\hbar\omega
\left(
n+\frac12
\right)
\]

</div>

<p>
The classical turning point satisfies:
</p>

<div class="equation">

\[
E_n=
\frac12m\omega^2x_t^2.
\]

</div>

<p>
Therefore:
</p>

<div class="equation">

\[
\boxed{
x_t=
\frac{\sqrt{2n+1}}{\alpha}
}
\]

</div>

<div class="equation">

\[
\alpha=
\sqrt{\frac{m\omega}{\hbar}}.
\]

</div>

</section>


<section class="section">

<div class="section-title">
Complete Solution
</div>


<div class="step">

<h4>Ground state n=0</h4>

<div class="equation">

\[
u_t=1
\]

</div>

<div class="equation">

\[
P_0=
\frac{2}{\sqrt{\pi}}
\int_1^\infty e^{-u^2}\,du
\]

</div>

<div class="final-equation">

\[
\boxed{
P_0=\operatorname{erfc}(1)=0.1573
}
\]

</div>

<p>
Therefore approximately 15.73\% lies beyond the classical
turning points.
</p>

</div>


<div class="step">

<h4>First excited state n=1</h4>

<div class="equation">

\[
u_t=\sqrt3
\]

</div>

<div class="equation">

\[
\psi_1(u)
=
\frac{\sqrt2}{\pi^{1/4}}
u e^{-u^2/2}
\]

</div>

<div class="equation">

\[
P_1
=
2
\int_{\sqrt3}^{\infty}
\frac{2u^2}{\sqrt{\pi}}
e^{-u^2}\,du
\]

</div>

<div class="final-equation">

\[
\boxed{
P_1=0.1116
}
\]

</div>

<p>
Approximately 11.16\%.
</p>

</div>


<div class="step">

<h4>Second excited state n=2</h4>

<div class="equation">

\[
u_t=\sqrt5
\]

</div>

<div class="equation">

\[
|\psi_2(u)|^2
=
\frac{1}{8\sqrt{\pi}}
(4u^2-2)^2e^{-u^2}
\]

</div>

<div class="equation">

\[
P_2=
2\int_{\sqrt5}^{\infty}
|\psi_2(u)|^2du
\]

</div>

<div class="final-equation">

\[
\boxed{
P_2=0.09507
}
\]

</div>

<p>
Approximately 9.51\%.
</p>

</div>


<div class="table-wrap">

<table>

<tr>
<th>State</th>
<th>Turning point</th>
<th>Probability outside</th>
</tr>

<tr>
<td>n=0</td>
<td>1/\alpha</td>
<td>15.73%</td>
</tr>

<tr>
<td>n=1</td>
<td>\sqrt3/\alpha</td>
<td>11.16%</td>
</tr>

<tr>
<td>n=2</td>
<td>\sqrt5/\alpha</td>
<td>9.51%</td>
</tr>

</table>

</div>

</section>


<div class="physics-box">

<h3>Physical Interpretation</h3>

<p>
Classically the particle cannot exist outside the turning points.
Quantum mechanically the wave function extends into the classically
forbidden region, producing a nonzero probability there.
</p>

</div>

</article>



